{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GEs2m3ZdR6BuncpieK9zyF","type":"tool_result","content":"Reviewed: 4a8e7f64ce4ee0bd2733a7829b7cd51ae872d859\n\nVerified: Independently read the specified three-dot authored diff, CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md, PROCESS.md, WI-902's full spec and handback.py SpecRef, rubric, and cited requirement/test/interface rows. Hunted silent successor misbinding, fail-open acceptance, and destructive dependency rewrites first. Valid adoption closes and sweeps without allocation; malformed, missing and wrong-lineage adoption refusals, drafted successors, mixed successor sets and replay idempotence passed the prescribed suite. Live amended rows equal their approval snapshots. The checkout remained unchanged. Two independently reproduced issues remain below.\n\nDone-when coverage:\n\n| Spec obligation | Coverage observed |\n| --- | --- |\n| Existing superseding successor satisfies close and sweep; empty early close refuses | test_an_adoption_of_a_superseding_row_closes_and_sweeps (partial and cancellation); existing empty-successor close/sweep tests. First-consumption versus replay distinction has the uncovered rejected-then-replayed case in F1. |\n| OI-73 successor exit remains mandatory | Empty-section refusals, invalid-adoption close cases and first-sweep open-state refusal passed; F1 bypasses that acceptance boundary on recovery. |\n| Missing/non-superseding rows refuse; drafted successor remains supported | test_an_adoption_that_is_not_accepted_refuses_the_close; test_the_sweep_accepts_an_adoption_only_of_a_superseding_row; test_a_drafted_successor_beside_an_adoption_still_mints. |\n| Amended sweep/close spine states the behavior and is adjudicated | SR-148, LLR-153/161/312/335 and TC-147/156/254/360 checked against code and existing related rows; acts 113-115 record re-attestation/approval and snapshots match. F1 and F2 prevent semantic endorsement of LLR-335 as written. |\n| One independent cross-family REVIEW-A | This independent full-lane review supplies the review; its verdict requests changes. |\n| Trunk-anchored RESYNC_PACK entry | Entry since 30d32823a; merge-base confirms that anchor is an ancestor of the trunk base; new adoption module is in bootstrap_manifest. |\n\nScoped semantic decisions: SR-148's existing selection, fail-closed and approval constraints remain sound; its additive adoption acceptance does not replace those constraints. Retain SR-144 and SR-220 acceptance and approval unchanged. Carry the replay correction into LLR-335/TC-360 under their applicable authority, preserving enduring close, consolidation and intake regression tests. For changed LLR-153/intake behavior and counterparts IF-090/IF-244, retain the function/return and CLI/exit schemas: the new successor choice uses those same surfaces. Retain IF-054's needs semantics and IF-101's allocation boundary: adoption changes dependency targets and allocates no identity, without changing either carrier.\n\nRetained child decisions/purposes: LLR-153/TC-147 own allocation identity, title dedup and the whole successor-set rewrite; further splitting this amendment would separate the identity decision from its required lineage effect. LLR-161/TC-156 own immutable close-event identity and continuation without reopening terminal work; splitting draft/adopt variants adds no independent event guarantee. LLR-312/TC-254 own consolidation preflight and preservation of all effects; its adoption refusal belongs to that existing atomic close decision. LLR-335/TC-360 own the single adoption acceptance decision across parsing, event identity, registry validation and both consumers; splitting those inputs into independent approvals would add no separate successor-acceptance purpose. These are semantic retention decisions, not a child-count judgment.\n\nCommands (Python is C:/Projects/ai-template/.venv/Scripts/python.exe; PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1):\n\n- git rev-parse HEAD; specified git diff ccd66391996fd1669d666e3f