{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvfjWwjwf5zoi43NZvCh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ruled finding DB as **FIX**, against the coordinator's contest. The verdict is `docs/reviews/wi-902/009-ADJUDICATE-653e96e.md`, committed as `eeaeed12d` with the `WI: WI-902` trailer; the commit checks passed.\n\n**The defect:** a later sweep decides whether an adoption was already consumed by checking one thing: did the disposition spec exist in `complete/` at the sweep's starting commit (`_landed_before`, `intake.py:1818`). That is also true after a landing sweep that *refused* the adoption. So the next plain `intake.py sweep` treats the refused adoption as consumed, checks lineage only, and re-points the dependents.\n\n**Reproduction:** I ran it myself with the suite's own real-git fixtures, outside this worktree:\n\n| Successor | Landing sweep | Plain sweep afterwards |\n|---|---|---|\n| complete | refused: \"names no open row\", WI-021 still needs WI-005 | exit 0, WI-021 now needs WI-020 |\n| cancelled | refused the same way | exit 0, WI-021 now waits on a cancelled row |\n\n**Why FIX:**\n- **It breaks approved text.** SR-148 accepts only \"an already-open item that supersedes it\", and LLR-335 allows the lineage-only replay only \"after the disposition was already consumed\". A refused adoption was never consumed.\n- **It breaks the row's main rule.** An early close must leave a live successor. Here a partial close ends up with none, and the refusal disappears on the next run of the documented recovery command.\n- **It's in scope.** It needs only content an agent writes into the repo, and no contrived host.\n- **D-001 doesn't settle it.** The decision does record this exact gap, but it handed the call to the next review, the owner hasn't seen it, and it's flagged high-risk. A delegated decision can't make code that contradicts two approved rows conformant.\n\n**On the remedy:** the coordinator objected that the reviewer's fix is D-001's rejected alternative, a new \"consumed\" marker. The fix doesn't need one. Whether the first consumption accepted the adoption can be worked out again from git. For example, apply the open-state rule to the tree where the disposition landed in `complete/`. That gives the same verdict as the landing sweep every time, and writes nothing. The lane can choose another mechanism, as long as replay depends on the first consumption having accepted.\n\nWhat the lane owes:\n- the new replay rule;\n- a test where the landing sweep refuses and a later sweep replays, for both complete and cancelled successors;\n- updated wording in LLR-335 and TC-360.\n\nThe coordinator should also mark D-001 as superseded by this ruling.\n\nI didn't touch the code, the review, the findings file, the registries or the untracked `.requested` file."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_read_input_tokens":256748,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":465},"output_tokens":728,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73ae5cf9-d335-4bb7-a08f-7631da597f26","uuid":"f2287148-56cd-4a72-90db-9c3ede2d265a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:15:02.073Z","request_id":"req_011CfvfjWDastv9FRb4ivLqB"}
